notes.their_explanation:
On the QE-filtering collapse, the only explanation is one sentence in Sec 4.1. The result "does not contradict" earlier filtering work, because that work never cut the data down to such a small fraction (0.3%). They then conclude that NewsPaLM's efficiency cannot be matched by selecting a high-quality subset of a large noisy corpus. This explanation is NOT tested:
- no sweep over the filtering fraction;
- no random-1M-of-WMT'23 baseline, so selection cannot be separated from size;
- no look at what the selected subset contains (source corpus or domain, duplicates, repetitiveness, length, vocabulary);
- a single run per condition.
A confound they do not discuss: the filtered set is sentence-level only, while the full WMT'23 baseline also contains blob-level data and the en-de test sets are paragraph-level. Their own Fig 2 shows sentence-only training breaks down on long segments. The de-en collapse (57.27 vs 80.55 for same-size sentence-level MBR) is harder to blame on length, because the paper flags only the en-de test sets as paragraph-level.
For other results they give hypotheses, some of them tested:
- Greedy data beats MBR data in pretraining, which they explain as MBR outputs being "more free-style" and harder to learn. They test this with the curriculum experiment (Table 5).
- Blob data matters less in fine-tuning because long-context skill learned in pretraining is not forgotten. This is a hypothesis only.

notes.relation_to_C1:
LEAVES OPEN. The paper neither establishes nor directly contradicts C1, but a reviewer could read its headline as contrary evidence.
(1) Over-selection of repetitive text. They never measure repetitiveness, n-gram overlap, duplicate rate or source-corpus share in the kept half. The closest point is qualitative (Sec. 5.1): templated sentences are abundant in WMT de-en data, and BleurtQE throws out templated pairs whose entity or number is mistranslated. That shows QE scores separate correct from wrong instances of a template. It says nothing either way about whether correctly instantiated templates are pushed into the top of the ranking. The Bible observation (Table 4d) shows that QE selection is not neutral to text type. That fits our premise that the top band shifts the source mix (77% UN), but they do not quantify it.
(2) Repetitiveness rather than source mix as the cause. Not addressed; they never control for source mix.
(3) Out-of-domain damage. In their setting the evidence points the other way: the QE-selected half helps on WMT general-domain sets and is roughly neutral on IWSLT TED and ACL-talk sets (Table 2). The one COMET22 loss against full data is small: ja->en IWSLT'23, -0.4 to -0.5. That setting differs from ours on every axis that matters:
- they keep the top 50% of 34-293M pairs; we keep about the top 6% (about 1M of 16.6M);
- they train a 551M model from scratch to convergence; we fine-tune a converged Transformer-base for 10k steps;
- they report COMET22; we report BLEU;
- their pools are pre-cleaned WMT data dominated by ParaCrawl; ours is en-fr with a UN-heavy top band.
C1 is compatible with Peter et al. only if it is explicitly scoped to the extreme top band used for fine-tuning.
